//? Title: AdaBoost on the Wisconsin Breast-Cancer Data
//? Subtitle: Exponential loss, the training-error bound and margins with datamunge's stats::AdaBoostClassifier
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
AdaBoost (Freund and Schapire, 1997) builds a strong classifier as a weighted
vote of *decision stumps*, one-split trees that are individually weak. We
derive the algorithm as greedy minimization of the exponential loss. From
that derivation follow the vote weights, the reweighting rule, the
training-error bound \(\prod_t 2\sqrt{\varepsilon_t(1-\varepsilon_t)}\), and
the fact that each stump is useless under the next round's weights. All four
are checked numerically. On the 569 tumours of the Wisconsin diagnostic data,
boosting takes the test error from 11% for one stump to 2.9%. The test error
keeps falling after the training error reaches zero, while the smallest
margin keeps growing, as margin theory predicts.
)

\toc

> The data

`dm::datasets::breast_cancer()` is the Wisconsin diagnostic breast-cancer
data (Street, Wolberg and Mangasarian, 1993). Each of 569 tumours is
described by 30 measurements of the cell nuclei in a digitized image of a
fine-needle aspirate: radius, texture, perimeter, area, smoothness,
compactness, concavity, concave points, symmetry and fractal dimension. Each
appears as its mean, its standard error and its "worst" (mean of the three
largest) value. The response is `diagnosis`, either `benign` or `malignant`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  const auto cancer = dm::datasets::breast_cancer();
  std::print("{} tumours, {} columns.\n\n", cancer.nrows(), cancer.ncols());
  std::print("{}\n", cancer
                       | dm::group_by("diagnosis")
                       | dm::summarise(agg::n("Tumours"), agg::mean("mean_radius", "Mean radius"),
                                       agg::mean("worst_perimeter", "Worst perimeter"),
                                       agg::mean("mean_concave_points", "Mean concave points"),
                                       agg::mean("worst_texture", "Worst texture"))
                       | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
