| benign | 107 |
// result_end
\caption(Class counts in the training and test sets)

> AdaBoost as greedy minimization of exponential loss

Code the classes as \(y\in\{-1,+1\}\), with `malignant` as \(+1\). A
*decision stump* \(h(x)=s\cdot\operatorname{sign}(c - x_j)\) predicts from a
single feature \(x_j\), a threshold \(c\) and a polarity \(s=\pm 1\). AdaBoost
builds an additive model

$$ F_T(x)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\alpha_t h_t(x),\qquad H(x)=\operatorname{sign}F_T(x), $$

one term at a time. We derive each term from the *exponential loss*
\(L(F)=\frac1n\sum_i e^{-y_iF(x_i)}\) (Friedman, Hastie and Tibshirani,
2000).

>> The vote weight

Suppose \(F_{t-1}\) is fixed and we add \(\alpha h\). Define the example
weights \(D_t(i)\propto e^{-y_iF_{t-1}(x_i)}\), normalized to sum to 1. Since
\(y_ih(x_i)=+1\) when \(h\) is right and \(-1\) when it is wrong,

$$ \sum_i D_t(i)\,e^{-\alpha y_ih(x_i)} = (1-\varepsilon)\,e^{-\alpha} + \varepsilon\,e^{\alpha} =: Z(\alpha,h), $$

where \(\varepsilon=\sum_i D_t(i)[h(x_i)\ne y_i]\) is the weighted error of
\(h\). For any \(\alpha>0\), \(Z\) increases with \(\varepsilon\), so the best
\(h\) is the stump with the smallest weighted error. Setting
\(\partial Z/\partial\alpha = -(1-\varepsilon)e^{-\alpha}+\varepsilon e^{\alpha}=0\)
gives

$$ \alpha_t=\tfrac12\ln\frac{1-\varepsilon_t}{\varepsilon_t},\qquad Z_t=2\sqrt{\varepsilon_t(1-\varepsilon_t)}. $$

The new weights are \(D_{t+1}(i)=D_t(i)\,e^{-\alpha_ty_ih_t(x_i)}/Z_t\): the
weights of misclassified examples grow by \(e^{\alpha_t}\) and those of the
rest shrink by \(e^{-\alpha_t}\).

>> The training-error bound

Unrolling the recursion from \(D_1(i)=1/n\) gives

$$ D_{T+1}(i)=\frac{e^{-y_iF_T(x_i)}}{n\prod_{t=1}^{T}Z_t}. $$

A misclassified example has \(y_iF_T(x_i)\le 0\), so
\([H(x_i)\ne y_i]\le e^{-y_iF_T(x_i)}\). Summing, and using
\(\sum_iD_{T+1}(i)=1\),

$$ \frac1n\sum_i[H(x_i)\ne y_i] \le \frac1n\sum_ie^{-y_iF_T(x_i)} = \prod_{t=1}^{T}Z_t. $$

Write \(\varepsilon_t=\tfrac12-\gamma_t\), where \(\gamma_t\) is the stump's
*edge* over chance. Then \(Z_t=\sqrt{1-4\gamma_t^2}\le e^{-2\gamma_t^2}\),
since \(1-u\le e^{-u}\), and so

$$ \text{training error}\le\exp\Big(-2\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma_t^2\Big). $$

Any fixed edge \(\gamma_t\ge\gamma>0\) drives the training error to zero
exponentially fast. The bound is crossed by the 0/1 error only when the
error reaches its floor of 0, which takes \(T > \ln n/(2\gamma^2)\) rounds.

>> Each stump is useless to the next round

Under the new weights, the stump just chosen has error exactly
\(\tfrac12\):

$$ \sum_{i:\,h_t(x_i)\ne y_i} D_{t+1}(i) = \frac{\varepsilon_te^{\alpha_t}}{Z_t} = \frac{\varepsilon_t\sqrt{(1-\varepsilon_t)/\varepsilon_t}}{2\sqrt{\varepsilon_t(1-\varepsilon_t)}} = \frac12. $$

So the next round never picks \(h_t\) again. AdaBoost reweights until the
last weak learner carries no information, and then looks for a new one.

> Fitting the model

`stats::AdaBoostClassifier` takes a data frame and a formula. Here
`diagnosis ~ .` uses all 30 measurements. `history()` has one row per round:
the stump's feature, threshold and polarity, its weighted error
\(\varepsilon_t\), vote \(\alpha_t\), \(Z_t\), the running bound
\(\prod_s Z_s\) and the training error.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::breast_cancer(), 0.3, 1, "diagnosis");
  const dm::stats::AdaBoostClassifier model(train, "diagnosis ~ .", {.n_rounds = 400});
  std::print("{} stumps, positive class '{}'.\n\n{}\n", model.n_rounds(), model.positive_class(),
             model.history().head(8).to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
400 stumps, positive class 'malignant'.

| round | feature | threshold | polarity | weak_error | alpha | Z | bound | train_error |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | worst_perimeter | 111.600 | -1 | 0.070 | 1.291 | 0.511 | 0.511 | 0.070 |
| 2 | mean_concave_points | 0.045 | -1 | 0.157 | 0.840 | 0.728 | 0.372 | 0.070 |
| 3 | worst_texture | 23.350 | -1 | 0.171 | 0.789 | 0.753 | 0.280 | 0.048 |
| 4 | worst_concave_points | 0.151 | -1 | 0.251 | 0.547 | 0.867 | 0.243 | 0.038 |
| 5 | mean_concave_points | 0.027 | -1 | 0.278 | 0.478 | 0.896 | 0.218 | 0.048 |
| 6 | area_error | 35.185 | -1 | 0.259 | 0.525 | 0.877 | 0.191 | 0.035 |
| 7 | worst_smoothness | 0.138 | -1 | 0.291 | 0.445 | 0.909 | 0.173 | 0.038 |
| 8 | mean_texture | 20.235 | -1 | 0.280 | 0.472 | 0.898 | 0.156 | 0.028 |
// result_end
\caption(The first eight boosting rounds; polarity \(-1\) predicts benign at or below the threshold)

The first stump, `worst_perimeter` \(\le 111.6\), already classifies 93% of
the training tumours. Later stumps are weaker, with \(\varepsilon_t\) near
0.3, and they get smaller votes. Round 2 picks a different stump, as the
identity above requires. The 0/1 training error is
not monotone, because it is the exponential loss, not the error, that each
round decreases.

The next block checks the three derived facts on the fitted model, using
only its public outputs. The weights \(D_{t+1}\) are recomputed from the
decision function \(F_t\), and the stump \(h_t\) is recovered as
\(\operatorname{sign}(F_t-F_{t-1})\).

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::breast_cancer(), 0.3, 1, "diagnosis");
  const dm::stats::AdaBoostClassifier model(train, "diagnosis ~ .", {.n_rounds = 400});
  const auto history = model.history();
  const auto& label = train.get_column<std::string>("diagnosis").values();
  std::vector<double> y;
  for (const auto& l : label) y.push_back(l == model.positive_class() ? 1.0 : -1.0);

  double worst_gap = -1.0;
  for (std::size_t t = 0; t < history.nrows(); ++t)
    worst_gap = std::max(worst_gap, history.double_at("train_error", t) - history.double_at("bound", t));
  std::print("max over rounds of (training error - prod Z): {:.3g}  (must be <= 0)\n", worst_gap);

  double edges = 0.0;
  for (double e : history.get_column<double>("weak_error").values()) edges += (0.5 - e) * (0.5 - e);
  std::print("prod Z = {:.3g} <= exp(-2 sum gamma^2) = {:.3g}\n", history.double_at("bound", history.nrows() - 1),
             std::exp(-2.0 * edges));

  // Error of h_t under D_{t+1} (D_{t+1} ~ exp(-y F_t)) for the first rounds: always 1/2.
  for (std::size_t t = 1; t <= 5; ++t) {
    // decision_function(data, 0) means "all rounds", so F_0 = 0 is written out.
    const auto before = t == 1 ? std::vector<double>(y.size(), 0.0) : model.decision_function(train, t - 1);
    const auto after = model.decision_function(train, t);
    double wrong = 0.0, total = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < y.size(); ++i) {
      const double w = std::exp(-y[i] * after[i]);
      const double h = after[i] > before[i] ? 1.0 : -1.0;
      total += w;
      if (h != y[i]) wrong += w;
    }
    std::print("round {}: weighted error of h_{} under D_{} = {:.12f}\n", t, t, t + 1, wrong / total);
  }
}
```
// result_begin:
// max over rounds of (training error - prod Z): -1.79e-11  (must be <= 0)
// prod Z = 1.79e-11 <= exp(-2 sum gamma^2) = 1.16e-10
// round 1: weighted error of h_1 under D_2 = 0.500000000000
// round 2: weighted error of h_2 under D_3 = 0.500000000000
// round 3: weighted error of h_3 under D_4 = 0.500000000000
// round 4: weighted error of h_4 under D_5 = 0.500000000000
// round 5: weighted error of h_5 under D_6 = 0.500000000000
// result_end

> Training error, test error and the bound

`staged_error()` evaluates the ensemble after every round. The left panel
shows the training error, the test error and the bound
\(\prod_tZ_t\) once it falls below 0.12. The right panel shows the
exponential decay of the bound on a log scale, together with the cruder
bound \(\exp(-2\sum\gamma_t^2)\).

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/adaboost_errors.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::breast_cancer(), 0.3, 1, "diagnosis");
  const dm::stats::AdaBoostClassifier model(train, "diagnosis ~ .", {.n_rounds = 400});
  const auto history = model.history();
  const auto rounds = history.get_column<double>("round").values();
  const auto test_error = model.staged_error(test);

  std::vector<double> bound_x, bound_y, log_bound, log_gamma;
  double edges = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t t = 0; t < rounds.size(); ++t) {
    const double b = history.double_at("bound", t), e = history.double_at("weak_error", t);
    edges += (0.5 - e) * (0.5 - e);
    if (b < 0.12) bound_x.push_back(rounds[t]), bound_y.push_back(b);
    log_bound.push_back(std::log10(b));
    log_gamma.push_back(-2.0 * edges / std::log(10.0));
  }
  auto errors = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  errors.lines(rounds, history.get_column<double>("train_error").values(), "training error", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(rounds, test_error, "test error", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .lines(bound_x, bound_y, "bound prod Z", dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 1.5)
      .title("Error rates by number of rounds")
      .x_label("boosting rounds T")
      .y_label("misclassification rate")
      .y_limits(0, 0.12);
  auto bounds = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  bounds.lines(rounds, log_bound, "prod Z", dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 2.0)
      .lines(rounds, log_gamma, "exp(-2 sum gamma^2)", dm::RGB{234, 88, 12}, 2.0)
      .title("The bound decays exponentially")
      .x_label("boosting rounds T")
      .y_label("log10 of the bound");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(errors).add(bounds).size(1200, 480).save_svg("build/adaboost_errors.svg");
  for (int t : {1, 10, 50, 100, 200, 400})
    std::print("T = {:3}: training error {:.4f}, test error {:.4f}, bound {:.3g}\n", t,
               history.double_at("train_error", t - 1), test_error[t - 1], history.double_at("bound", t - 1));
}
```
// result_begin:
// T =   1: training error 0.0704, test error 0.1111, bound 0.511
// T =  10: training error 0.0201, test error 0.0585, bound 0.121
// T =  50: training error 0.0000, test error 0.0351, bound 0.00615
// T = 100: training error 0.0000, test error 0.0351, bound 0.000308
// T = 200: training error 0.0000, test error 0.0292, bound 1.01e-06
// T = 400: training error 0.0000, test error 0.0292, bound 1.79e-11
// \image(figures/adaboost_errors.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Left: training and test error with the bound \(\prod Z_t\); right: the bound and \(\exp(-2\sum\gamma_t^2)\) on a log scale)
\alttext(
Two panels. Left: the blue training-error curve falls from 0.07 to zero by
about round 40; the red test-error curve falls from 0.11 to about 0.03 and
keeps drifting down after the training error is zero; the grey bound curve
falls from 0.12 towards zero above the training error. Right: both bounds
fall as nearly straight lines on the log scale, the orange cruder bound
above the grey one.
)

The bound holds at every round and decays at a steady exponential rate: the
right panel is almost a straight line. The striking part is the test error.
It keeps decreasing from 3.5% at 50 rounds, where the training error is
already zero, to 2.9% at 200 rounds. Adding more stumps does not overfit
here, which the training error alone cannot explain.

> Margins

Schapire, Freund, Bartlett and Lee (1998) explain this through the
*normalized margin*

$$ m_i = \frac{y_iF_T(x_i)}{\sum_t\alpha_t}\in[-1,1], $$

positive exactly when example \(i\) is classified correctly, and large when
the vote is confident. Their bound on the test error decreases in the
fraction of training examples with small margin, and it does not grow with
\(T\). After the training error reaches zero, AdaBoost keeps pushing up the
smallest margins, because the exponential loss still rewards it.
`margins()` returns \(m_i\) for each training row.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/adaboost_margins.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::breast_cancer(), 0.3, 1, "diagnosis");
  const dm::stats::AdaBoostClassifier model(train, "diagnosis ~ .", {.n_rounds = 400});
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, dm::RGB>> stages{
      {5, {234, 88, 12}}, {50, {22, 163, 74}}, {400, {37, 99, 235}}};
  for (const auto& [T, color] : stages) {
    auto m = model.margins(train, T);
    std::ranges::sort(m);
    std::vector<double> share(m.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m.size(); ++i) share[i] = (i + 1.0) / m.size();
    plot.lines(m, share, std::format("T = {}", T), color, 2.0);
    std::print("T = {:3}: smallest margin {:+.3f}, 5% quantile {:+.3f}, median {:+.3f}\n", T, m.front(),
               m[m.size() / 20], m[m.size() / 2]);
  }
  plot.abline_v(0.0)
      .x_limits(-0.4, 1.25)
      .title("Cumulative distribution of training margins")
      .x_label("normalized margin y F(x) / sum(alpha)")
      .y_label("fraction of training tumours")
      .size(820, 500)
      .save_svg("build/adaboost_margins.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// T =   5: smallest margin -0.358, 5% quantile +0.054, median +0.722
// T =  50: smallest margin +0.081, 5% quantile +0.154, median +0.391
// T = 400: smallest margin +0.155, 5% quantile +0.163, median +0.319
// \image(figures/adaboost_margins.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Empirical distribution functions of the training margins after 5, 50 and 400 rounds; points left of the vertical line are misclassified)
\alttext(
Three S-shaped cumulative curves. The orange curve for 5 rounds starts left
of zero; the green curve for 50 rounds starts just right of zero; the blue
curve for 400 rounds starts furthest right, near 0.15, and is the steepest.
)

From 50 to 400 rounds, the smallest margin roughly doubles. The median
margin falls, because AdaBoost gives up confidence on easy examples to gain
it on the hard ones. The curve moves away from zero at the bottom, which is
the part the margin bound depends on.

> Comparison and feature importance

A single stump has a test error of 11%. A linear discriminant analysis on
all 30 features, which models the classes as Gaussians with a common
covariance, is a strong baseline for this data.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::breast_cancer(), 0.3, 1, "diagnosis");
  const dm::stats::AdaBoostClassifier model(train, "diagnosis ~ .", {.n_rounds = 400});
  const dm::stats::LDA lda(train, "diagnosis ~ .");
  const auto predicted = lda.predict(test);
  const auto& truth = test.get_column<std::string>("diagnosis").values();
  double lda_wrong = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < truth.size(); ++i) lda_wrong += predicted[i] != truth[i];

  std::print("| Model | Training error | Test error |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t T : {1, 10, 50, 400})
    std::print("| AdaBoost, {} stump{} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", T, T == 1 ? "" : "s", model.error(train, T),
               model.error(test, T));
  std::print("| LDA, 30 features | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n\n", 1.0 - lda.training_accuracy(), lda_wrong / truth.size());
  std::print("{}\n", model.feature_importance().head(8) | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Model | Training error | Test error |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| AdaBoost, 1 stump | 0.070 | 0.111 |
| AdaBoost, 10 stumps | 0.020 | 0.058 |
| AdaBoost, 50 stumps | 0.000 | 0.035 |
| AdaBoost, 400 stumps | 0.000 | 0.029 |
| LDA, 30 features | 0.033 | 0.029 |

| feature | importance |
| :--- | ---: |
| mean_concave_points | 0.084 |
| mean_compactness | 0.083 |
| worst_texture | 0.078 |
| area_error | 0.074 |
| worst_smoothness | 0.053 |
| fractal_dimension_error | 0.051 |
| mean_smoothness | 0.051 |
| compactness_error | 0.049 |
// result_end
\caption(Test error of boosted stumps against LDA, and the eight features with the largest share of the total vote \(\sum\alpha_t\))

Boosting reaches LDA's test error of 2.9% (5 of 171 test tumours). The
perimeter stump of round 1 has the largest single vote, but over 400 rounds
the votes spread over many features. Concavity, compactness, texture and
smoothness measurements carry the most total weight.

> What the boundary looks like

With two features the decision regions can be drawn exactly. Every stump
cuts along an axis, so \(F_T\) is constant on the cells of the grid formed by
the thresholds. `plot_decision_regions()` shades each cell by its predicted
class. The boundary is a staircase, and with enough stumps it can bend
around the data as needed.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/adaboost_regions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto [train, test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::breast_cancer(), 0.3, 1, "diagnosis");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (std::size_t T : {3, 100}) {
    const dm::stats::AdaBoostClassifier model(train, "diagnosis ~ worst_perimeter + mean_concave_points",
                                              {.n_rounds = T});
    std::print("T = {:3}: training error {:.3f}, test error {:.3f}\n", T, model.error(train), model.error(test));
    layout.add(model.plot_decision_regions(test).title(std::format("{} stumps (test tumours shown)", T)));
  }
  layout.size(1200, 520).save_svg("build/adaboost_regions.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// T =   3: training error 0.070, test error 0.111
// T = 100: training error 0.040, test error 0.111
// \image(figures/adaboost_regions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Decision regions of AdaBoost on two features after 3 and 100 rounds, with the test tumours; blue is benign, red malignant)
\alttext(
Two panels with the same axes as the earlier scatter plot. With 3 stumps the
plane is split into a few rectangles by an L-shaped boundary. With 100
stumps the boundary is a fine staircase that follows the overlap between the
blue and red points more closely.
)

On these two features alone, 100 stumps fit the training data better than
3 (4.0% against 7.0% training error) but leave the test error at 11.1%. The
extra steps of the staircase chase single training points. With all 30 features the margins matter more than the
shape of any one boundary.

> Summary

- AdaBoost is forward-stagewise minimization of the exponential loss. The vote \(\alpha_t=\frac12\ln\frac{1-\varepsilon_t}{\varepsilon_t}\), the reweighting rule, the bound \(\prod_t 2\sqrt{\varepsilon_t(1-\varepsilon_t)}\le e^{-2\sum\gamma_t^2}\) and the identity "\(h_t\) has error \(\frac12\) under \(D_{t+1}\)" all follow, and all hold on the fitted model to machine precision.
- On the Wisconsin data, 400 stumps take the test error from 11.1% for a single stump to 2.9%, level with LDA.
- The test error keeps improving after the training error reaches zero, while the smallest training margin keeps increasing.

> References

- Y. Freund, R. E. Schapire (1997). A decision-theoretic generalization of on-line learning and an application to boosting. *Journal of Computer and System Sciences* 55(1), 119\-\-139.
- J. Friedman, T. Hastie, R. Tibshirani (2000). Additive logistic regression: a statistical view of boosting. *Annals of Statistics* 28(2), 337\-\-407.
- R. E. Schapire, Y. Freund, P. Bartlett, W. S. Lee (1998). Boosting the margin: a new explanation for the effectiveness of voting methods. *Annals of Statistics* 26(5), 1651\-\-1686.
- W. N. Street, W. H. Wolberg, O. L. Mangasarian (1993). Nuclear feature extraction for breast tumor diagnosis. *Proc. SPIE* 1905, 861\-\-870.
